call_details.response:
The board contains a critical structural feature: Black (x) has a forced winning sequence beginning with a placement in column 6. After \(\mathrm{x}\) plays at \((4,6)\), the square \((3,6)\) becomes an immediate winning threat (horizontal completion of the three \(\mathrm{x}\) already present on row 3). The forced block at \((3,6)\) then leaves \((2,6)\) open, which completes a second diagonal of four \(\mathrm{x}\). Consequently any move that leaves this sequence intact loses.

The unique square that neutralizes the sequence is \((0,3)\). Occupying it turns \((3,6)\) into a winning terminal square for White as well (the diagonal \((0,3)\)-\((1,4)\)-\((2,5)\)-\((3,6)\)). Hence \(\mathrm{x}\) can never safely open column 6; the same square is also already a terminal threat for Black, so the first player to open the column simply hands the opponent an immediate win. Column 1 is likewise poisoned for both sides (the square \((2,1)\) simultaneously completes a horizontal four for Black and a diagonal four for White). The only remaining legal non-losing files are therefore 0, 2, 4 and 5, together with the four empty cells of column 0—exactly seven safe squares.

With those seven squares the second player (White) possesses a simple pairing strategy:
- the three top-row cells \(\{(0,2),(0,4),(0,5)\}\) together with the already-placed stone at \((0,3)\) form a horizontal four;
- Black can be forced either to surrender that four or to spend a tempo inside the three-cell set;
- after Black’s tempo is spent, White still possesses enough moves to answer any attempt by Black to build a vertical four in column 0;
- the total number of safe cells is odd and it is Black’s turn, so Black is ultimately forced to open a poisoned file and White wins on the spot.

The same seven-cell end-game (and therefore the same win) is reached after the forcing moves 2 or 5 followed by the defensive placement 3. All three columns are consequently winning. Column 3 is the most direct of them: it realises the key defensive stone at once, keeps the initiative, and is the central file.

Final Answer: 3

